    {
      "id": "a5ic4as",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "HIGH COURT"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The High Court stands at the head of a state's judicial administration with specific constitutional provisions defining its authority.",
        "Under Article 226, High Courts possess broad writ jurisdiction for the enforcement of fundamental rights and other legal rights.",
        "Article 227 confers supervisory jurisdiction to the High Court over all subordinate courts and tribunals within its territorial limits.",
        "As a Court of Record under Article 215, the High Court retains the power to punish for contempt of itself.",
        "Judges of the High Court are governed by constitutional rules regarding appointment, qualifications, tenure, removal, transfer, and salaries."
      ],
      "id": "6wmupss"
    },
